<article class="env-lemma" id="01ME">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01ME">Lemma <span data-tag="01ME">27.8.10</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a graded ring. The scheme $\text{Proj}(S)$ has a canonical morphism towards the affine scheme $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(S_0)$, agreeing with the map on topological spaces coming from Algebra, Definition <a href="/tag/00JN" data-tag="00JN">10.57.1</a>. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We saw above that our construction of $\widetilde S$, resp. $\widetilde M$ gives a sheaf of $S_0$-algebras, resp. $S_0$-modules. Hence we get a morphism by Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01I1" data-tag="01I1">26.6.4</a>. This morphism, when restricted to $D_{+}(f)$ comes from the canonical ring map $S_0 \to S_{(f)}$. The maps $S \to S_ f$, $S_{(f)} \to S_ f$ are $S_0$-algebra maps, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01M4" data-tag="01M4">27.8.1</a>. Hence if the homogeneous prime $\mathfrak p \subset S$ corresponds to the $\mathbf{Z}$-graded prime $\mathfrak p' \subset S_ f$ and the (usual) prime $\mathfrak p'' \subset S_{(f)}$, then each of these has the same inverse image in $S_0$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>